/* ============================================================================
   perfect-numbers-below.epher -- the perfect numbers below 10000

   Searches for perfect numbers below 10000 with the Euclid-Euler
   theorem: every even perfect number has the form 2^(p - 1) * (2^p - 1)
   with 2^p - 1 prime (Euclid, Elements IX.36, and Euler), and no odd
   perfect number is known at all (the smallest possible one exceeds
   10^1500, Ochem and Rao, 2012). So below 10000 the search is over the
   Mersenne primes 2^p - 1 for p up to 13, and it finds exactly four
   numbers. A definitional check then confirms each find: its proper
   divisor sum equals the number itself.

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 16, section 16.8; the odd-perfect bound is P. Ochem and M. Rao,
   "Odd perfect numbers are greater than 10^1500", Math. Comp. 81 (2012).
   All results are exact.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/perfect-numbers-below.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/perfect-numbers-below.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the bound 10000 is the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

/* ---- the Euclid-Euler search -------------------------------------------------------------
   v = {p, perfect numbers found}; p = 13 gives 2^12 * 8191 = 33550336,
   already beyond the bound, so p up to 13 with a bound test covers all
   even perfects below 10000. */
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] <= 13 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(2 ^ v[1] - 1) and 2 ^ (v[1] - 1) * (2 ^ v[1] - 1) < 10000 then 1 else 0)}
print("perfect numbers below 10000:", v[2])         // 4: 6, 28, 496, 8128

// ---- the Mersenne primes behind the finds ---------------------------------------------------
for p in {2, 3, 5, 7} do isprime(2 ^ p - 1)         // 3, 7, 31, 127 all prime
print("6 = 2^1 * 3:", 2 ^ 1 * 3)
print("28 = 2^2 * 7:", 2 ^ 2 * 7)
print("496 = 2^4 * 31:", 2 ^ 4 * 31)
print("8128 = 2^6 * 127:", 2 ^ 6 * 127)

// ---- definitional check: proper divisor sums ------------------------------------------------
// pairdiv(n, i) adds each divisor of n with its partner n / i (a square
// adds its root once), so pairdiv(n, 1) is sigma(n); ps subtracts n.
def pairdiv(n, i) = if i * i > n then 0 else if mod(n, i) == 0 then (if i * i == n then i + pairdiv(n, i + 1) else i + n / i + pairdiv(n, i + 1)) else pairdiv(n, i + 1)
def sigma(n) = pairdiv(n, 1)
def ps(n) = sigma(n) - n
for x in {6, 28, 496} do ps(x) == x                 // proper sum equals n
// 8128: sigma(8128) = 16256 by the same pair walk, one divisor at a time.
v = {1, 0}
while v[1] * v[1] <= 8128 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if mod(8128, v[1]) == 0 then (if v[1] * v[1] == 8128 then v[1] else v[1] + 8128 / v[1]) else 0)}
print("proper divisor sum of 8128:", v[2] - 8128)   // 8128: it is perfect
print("8128 is perfect:", v[2] - 8128 == 8128)
/* ---- expected output ----
= {2, 0}
= perfect numbers below 10000: 4
= {true, true, true, true}
= 6 = 2^1 * 3: 6
= 28 = 2^2 * 7: 28
= 496 = 2^4 * 31: 496
= 8128 = 2^6 * 127: 8128
= {true, true, true}
= {1, 0}
= proper divisor sum of 8128: 8128
= 8128 is perfect: true
*/
